UPDATE visits SET diagnosis = 'I49.3 Premature ventricular contractions' WHERE id = 'd8feaacc-fb7a-4c21-8cc0-46b337cc4b8c';
UPDATE visits SET raw_notes = '{"isDetailed":true,"quickSummary":"Patient presents with 3-week history of heart palpitations. EKG shows PVCs. Started on Propranolol 40mg BID.","historyOfPresentIllness":"Mr. Johnson is a 40-year-old male presenting with a 3-week history of intermittent heart palpitations described as a \"fluttering\" sensation in the chest. Episodes occur 5-10 times daily, lasting seconds to minutes, and are not associated with syncope, chest pain, or dyspnea. He reports increased stress at work and higher caffeine intake recently (4-5 cups of coffee/day). No prior cardiac history. No family history of sudden cardiac death. Non-smoker, occasional alcohol use.","reportedSymptoms":"Constitutional: No fever, weight changes. Cardiovascular: Palpitations as noted, no chest pain, no edema. Respiratory: No dyspnea, no cough. GI: No nausea, normal appetite. Neurological: No dizziness, no syncope.","physicalExamination":"General: Well-appearing male in no acute distress. Vitals: HR 78, BP 128/82, RR 16, SpO2 99% on RA. Cardiovascular: Regular rate with occasional ectopy, no murmurs, no gallops. Lungs: Clear bilaterally. Extremities: No edema, pulses 2+ bilaterally.","assessment":"Premature ventricular contractions (PVCs) - frequent, symptomatic. Likely benign given preserved ejection fraction and normal echocardiogram. Contributing factors include elevated caffeine intake and psychosocial stress.","plan":["Start Propranolol 40mg PO BID with meals for symptomatic PVC suppression.","Reduce caffeine intake to 1 cup/day maximum.","Lifestyle modifications: regular exercise, stress management techniques.","Patient education on PVCs provided - reassurance that condition is typically benign with normal heart structure.","Order 24-hour Holter monitor in 3 months to quantify PVC burden on therapy."],"followUp":"2 weeks for blood pressure check and medication tolerance assessment. 3 months for repeat Holter monitor and cardiology follow-up.","testResults":[{"name":"Blood pressure panel","status":"Normal","value":"128/82 mmHg","date":"Feb 15"},{"name":"12-Lead electrocardiogram","status":"Normal","value":"Normal sinus rhythm with frequent PVCs. No ST-segment changes. Normal axis.","date":"Feb 15"},{"name":"Echocardiography","status":"Normal","value":"EF 55%, normal wall motion, no valvular abnormalities.","date":"Feb 15"}],"medicationsPrescribed":[{"name":"Propranolol 40mg tablet","instruction":"40 mg · Every 12 hours with meals","notes":"Take with food. Do not stop abruptly. Monitor heart rate and blood pressure."}],"transcript":[{"time":"00:00","speaker":"DR. GUPTA","text":"Good morning, Amit. I''m Dr. Gupta, one of the cardiologists here at City Heart Clinic. Have a seat. So, your primary care doctor referred you - tell me what''s been going on."},{"time":"00:12","speaker":"PATIENT","text":"Hi, doctor. Yeah, so about two weeks ago I started feeling these... I don''t know how to describe it... like my heart skips a beat? Or sometimes it feels like it''s doing an extra beat. It''s this weird fluttering in my chest."},{"time":"00:28","speaker":"DR. GUPTA","text":"Okay. And when you say two weeks, was there a specific moment it started, or did it come on gradually?"},{"time":"00:34","speaker":"PATIENT","text":"It kind of came on gradually. I noticed it first when I was lying in bed at night, trying to fall asleep. Then I started noticing it during the day too, especially when I''m sitting at my desk at work."},{"time":"00:47","speaker":"DR. GUPTA","text":"How often would you say it happens? A few times a day, a few times an hour?"},{"time":"00:52","speaker":"PATIENT","text":"Some days it''s just a few times. Other days, like yesterday, it felt like it was happening every few minutes. That''s when I got worried and called my doctor."}]}' WHERE id = 'd8feaacc-fb7a-4c21-8cc0-46b337cc4b8c';
